<!--
    A value a sign-sensitive operation reads keeps its own variable instead of
    merging into a global it is stored to, and the store stays where the binary
    makes it (P3 kuna_globalstorekeep, P6 kuna_globalvalue).

    Merge joins a register value with the global it is copied to.  The joined
    value then prints as a read of the global, and the operation on it takes the
    global's signedness.  For

        else { u = init * 3; sink = u; r = (int)(u >> 4); }

    kuna used to print `sink = init * 3; r = sink >> 4;`.  The shift is right
    only while `sink` is unsigned, which is how kuna types it, but kuna never
    declares the global, so a reader who gives `sink` its real type
    (`volatile int`) turns the logical shift arithmetic: for init 0x80000000 the
    rebuilt program returns -134217728 where the binary returns 134217728.  The
    same holds for a widening, a conversion to double and a byte compare, and
    for an operation that reads the value through `+` or `^` (`w_sadd`,
    `w_xordiv`, `w_f`, `w_cond`): C gives `sink + 1` the global's type too.
    A short or byte compare against a constant counts even when the constant
    only reaches the value later: a fold moves it across `+`, `-`, `~` or
    `^` (`w_fold16`: `u + 1 == 0` becomes `u == 0xffff`), after the store's
    fate is decided, and kuna printed `hsink = a0 * 3; v1 = hsink == -1;`.
    RulePropagateCopy keeps the store's COPY where the binary makes it and Merge
    refuses the copy-shadow join, so the value keeps its own type and the store
    keeps its place, ahead of a later pointer store that may alias the global.

    The wrapper only supplies the harness architecture (`load file` needs one).
    The pass loads globalstore_gcc_O0_x86_64 (built from globalstore_x86_64.c
    beside it).
-->
